1
00:00:09,390 --> 00:00:13,572
Let's just level set and understand what a Terraform provider is and how it works.

2
00:00:15,150 --> 00:00:18,400
So if you're watching this, you probably have used Terraform.

3
00:00:18,734 --> 00:00:28,004
You've written some infrastructure as code, you've used Terraform to plan, apply, and destroy your infrastructure, and you've done that with any number of cloud providers.

4
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:39,876
And you may wonder how does Terraform know how to provision infrastructure on, I think there's about 3,000 different providers in the registry.

5
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:43,812
So how does Terraform itself know to manage all of that cloud stuff?

6
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:47,044
Well, the provider is the secret sauce.

7
00:00:49,102 --> 00:00:52,800
So under the hood, a Terraform provider acts essentially as a plugin.

8
00:00:53,280 --> 00:01:00,772
It communicates with Terraform Core over RPC and presents the resources and data sources that you can manage with the provider.

9
00:01:01,360 --> 00:01:16,324
Terraform providers like Terraform itself are written in Go, and they themselves are going to rely on a client library, which is the piece of code that's going to communicate over HTTPS with your target API.

10
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:35,200
And the client library will handle the details of doing things like connecting and authenticating, maybe handling some of those low-level errors like an HTTP connection error, and other things like marshaling the data that it gets back in whatever format from whatever the API is.

11
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:49,760
And then the provider itself is going to use some standard Terraform libraries that we're going to be looking at today to communicate back to Terraform to, again, say, "Here's the resources.

12
00:01:49,870 --> 00:01:52,996
here's the state that we got from the API call.

13
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:58,436
here's what happened when we created your cloud resource and the results of that.

14
00:02:00,974 --> 00:02:04,836
So again, that's a very, very high-level flyover.

15
00:02:05,040 --> 00:02:08,515
but we're going to dive into the lab and talk about how to actually do it.

16
00:02:09,039 --> 00:02:13,440
So how and why would you create a provider?

17
00:02:13,998 --> 00:02:18,788
I think there's probably three main use cases if you're considering creating your own Terraform provider.

18
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:22,480
You may want to update an existing provider.

19
00:02:22,720 --> 00:02:36,640
As I mentioned earlier, there's 3,500 plus providers in the registry, and each of those providers is working with the upstream cloud provider that's adding new features, capabilities, making changes.

20
00:02:36,910 --> 00:02:38,640
and of course, all code has bugs.

21
00:02:38,766 --> 00:02:45,380
so there might be bug fixes and updates that you could contribute to an existing provider that way.

22
00:02:45,840 --> 00:02:50,980
So the first step of knowing that is knowing how providers work and knowing how that code works.

23
00:02:51,680 --> 00:03:00,612
One thing I want to note here is that the framework that I'm going to be using today is the newest framework that Terraform offers.

24
00:03:01,614 --> 00:03:07,520
Existing providers may still be may still be using an older framework called SDK V2.

25
00:03:07,950 --> 00:03:19,300
And it's just got some different semantics and some ways of handling data that are maybe not so ideal, which is why we created the framework in the first place.

26
00:03:20,080 --> 00:03:26,052
But conceptually, SDK V2 providers are going to work essentially the same way as framework providers.

27
00:03:26,318 --> 00:03:34,596
It's just something to be aware of if you're tackling an existing provider that it may not be using the exact same library that we're going to look at today.

28
00:03:35,726 --> 00:03:41,380
The other case that you might think of is you're ready to create and publish a new provider.

29
00:03:41,680 --> 00:03:49,412
Maybe your company is doing a new product launch and you want to support your customers to be able to manage that product with Terraform.

30
00:03:49,840 --> 00:04:01,540
Or maybe you're working with an open source project that doesn't yet have a Terraform provider, and so you're ready to create one again to manage the resources that that open source project provides.

31
00:04:01,966 --> 00:04:15,908
And then the third case, I think, is a lot of companies now are having internal applications that provision cloud resources or private cloud resources within your own data center, maybe.

32
00:04:16,240 --> 00:04:25,120
And so you can develop a custom provider to allow you to manage or to allow your users, your end users, to manage those applications with Terraform.

33
00:04:25,454 --> 00:04:30,244
And then you can use Terraform's private registry to distribute those providers internally.

34
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:34,916
Again, for something that's not available, widely available externally.

